  You are maybe 1/4 of the way through the game.  There are so 
many ways to play the game and so many interactions between 
activities and objects that general advice is the best, and may
still mislead you.


  There are a number of major goals remaining for you.

>Let me say before continuing that I don't like to use those big spoiler
>documents.  I prefer to learn by experience and from getting bits and
>pieces from other people.  So I'm just going to ask somewhat specific
>questions here.

  That's a long hard way to learn the game.  Just so you know.
You may want to get in some consultations with the well-known 
in-game entity.  NB: Costs zorkmids.

>
>I want to read blessed scrolls of identify until I get that big lucky
>break.  I've managed to bless and read two so far, but no luck.
>!oConfusion and Water have been really scarce and I have only two more
>identify scrolls at my disposal, both cursed.  How should I go about
>this?  Is this even an important goal right now?  It seems like a good
>idea to know what your tricks are before things get hairy.

  You can only carry so much.  Remember, you'll also want to be
able to pick up things that you find.  Imho it's better to have 
two or three escape items you can rely on than to try and 
carry/remember everything at once.  

  Complete identification is a simple and obvious goal, but not 
worth it for the most part.  There is no such thing as an 
insta-win item.  Plus, you'll keep finding new, unidentified
items.  A spellbook can help a lot here.

  (NB: There are a lot of other ways to identify items besides
scrolls of identify.  Most of them require that you be thoroughly
spoiled.)

>
>I also have some nice, but damaged, pieces of armor: a +4 cloak
>(orcish?), +3 elven boots, and a +2 helmet.  The cloak and boots are
>burnt; the cloak is down to +3.  

  "...the cloak is down to +3."   How did that happen?

>The helmet is thoroughly rusty.  I
>haven't found any oil so far.  Is it possible to repair them?  

  Yes, they can be repaired.  It's rarely worth it.  Even your 
highest enchantment item probably isn't worth it, due to its 
nature.  (You're *very* unlikely to discover the method of 
repairing items on your own.)
  
  Since you're so early in the game, you're going to see a lot 
more armor to check for enchantment.  You've done an excellent 
job of this so far.  Just keep doing it.  

  When you find better armor, you may want to consider not
wearing it until you can protect it.  That depends on a) whether
you ever find out how to protect armor and b) how much difference
the new armor will make.

>I'm
>guessing that grease would protect the helmet for the future, right?

  Helmets in general, yes.  I don't think your current helm has 
any base AC left to lose.   If so, you're just getting 2 points 
of AC from it.


>How can I protect the burnable items?  

  That's a very spoily question, with a lot of aspects to 
consider.  

>What's the best way to deal with
>pyrolisks?  (I just want a hint for the last question.)

  There are several (many?) ways of dealing with pyrolisks.
They're supposed to resemble the Ravenous Bugblatter beast
in at least one important aspect.

>
>My large cat has died.  I was otherwise occupied when it happened,
>figuring it could handle a leather golem.  

  Did you check the leather armor left by the golem for 
enchantment?

>Anyway, I haven't really
>missed having a pet, except when I would like buc status on an item I
>just found.  Should I bother getting a new one?  

  Normal pets (cats, dogs, horses, etc.) will still be able to 
help you by fighting for a while yet.  Knowing the buc status of 
an item is probably the best use for a pet at this point.  Unless
you find a better way.


